pagewire's change to pappl-retrofit 1.0b3: run each job's filter chain in a
child process of its own, and reap what the chain leaves behind.

_prFilter() called cfFilterChain() (libcupsfilters 2.1.1) in the job thread.
cfFilterChain() waits for its filter processes with wait(), which reaps any
child of the process. Two jobs printing at once on two queues of one printer
application each reap the other's filters; once its own children are gone,
wait() fails with ECHILD and the loop retries for good. Both jobs stay in
"processing" and the application spins at full CPU.

With this patch _prFilter() runs the chain through _prFilterChain(): it forks
one child, which runs cfFilterChain() and exits with its result, and waits for
that child alone with waitpid(). The filters are children of that child only,
so no other thread can reap them, and its wait() sees no other job's
processes. _prRasterPrepareJob() already ran the chain in a child
(cfFilterPOpen()); both now run _prFilterChainAndReap() there, which makes the
child a child subreaper and, after the chain, waits until every process the
chain left behind has ended (the CUPS backend that the "Backend" filter
starts). The application is PID 1 in its container, so without this each job
left one zombie: the stray wait() above used to reap them by accident.
That stray wait() also reaped processes orphaned outside a chain, such as a
backend left by a device listing that _prCUPSDevList() kills after its
timeout; those now stay zombies for the life of the container.
shortcut: no init process reaps them; run the application under one (tini as
PID 1) once such zombies pile up.

The patch also serializes the walk over the shared spooling-conversion array
in _prFilter(): a CUPS array keeps its iteration position in itself, so two
jobs looking up their pre-filter at once could miss it ("No pre-filter found
for input format ...") and abort.

Upstream report, open and unfixed:
https://github.com/OpenPrinting/pappl-retrofit/issues/36
Drop this patch, images/driver/filter-chain-test.c and the filter chain test
step in images/driver/Dockerfile once that issue is fixed: when pappl-retrofit
no longer calls cfFilterChain() in a job thread, or cfFilterChain() waits for
its own processes only, and the chain's leftover processes are reaped.

